**Polymorphism** means "many forms": the same name or interface behaves differently depending on context. In Java it appears in two ways.

**Compile-time (static) polymorphism: method overloading.** Several methods share a name but differ in parameter list (number, type or order). The compiler picks which one to call from the *declared types of the arguments*, before the program runs.

**Runtime (dynamic) polymorphism: method overriding.** A subclass provides its own version of a superclass method with the same signature. Which version runs is decided at run time from the *actual object type*, using dynamic dispatch.

```java
class Calc {
    int add(int a, int b)       { return a + b; }        // overload 1
    double add(double a, double b) { return a + b; }     // overload 2
}
class Animal {
    void sound() { System.out.println("Some sound"); }
}
class Dog extends Animal {
    @Override
    void sound() { System.out.println("Bark"); }
}
public class Demo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Calc c = new Calc();
        System.out.println(c.add(2, 3));      // compiler picks add(int,int)
        System.out.println(c.add(2.5, 3.5));  // compiler picks add(double,double)

        Animal a = new Dog();                 // reference type Animal, object Dog
        a.sound();                            // prints "Bark" (decided at run time)
    }
}
```

**Why one is compile time and the other runtime.** For `c.add(2, 3)` the argument types are known from the source, so the compiler binds the call to `add(int,int)` (early binding). For `a.sound()` the compiler only knows the reference type is `Animal`; the object could be any subclass, so the JVM looks at the real object's class when the call executes (late binding), which gives `Bark`.

**Key differences.**
- Overloading: same class (or inherited), different parameters, return type alone cannot distinguish; overriding: different classes, same signature.
- Static, private and final methods are not overridden; fields are not polymorphic.
- Overriding enables programming to an interface or base type and swapping implementations.

**Tip.** Mention the benefit: new subclasses plug in without changing callers.
